  "query_preview": "..\nToday and tonight: An atmospheric river aimed at Vancouver Island\nand southern British Columbia will sag south over northern \nWashington into the North ID Panhandle this morning and then\nremain nearly stationary into the afternoon. Strong westerly flow\ninto the Cascade crest combined with the increase in moisture \nwill result in steady rains into the west slopes of the Cascades,\nwith some precipitation spilling over the crest into the upper\nreaches of the Methow Valley, Lake Chelan, and Lake Wenatchee.\nSome dry air initially combined with a strong rain shadow east of\nthe Cascades will result in mainly dry conditions except for areas\nof light rain in the mountains of NE Washington into the ID\nPanhandle. This system will also bring in a 130 kt upper jet into\nsouthern British Columbia. This will keep our region on the\nsouthern, and thus stable side of the jet which will limit our\nmixing potential. Yet as 850mb winds increase to 20-30 kts out of\nthe southwest windy conditions will occur in the mountains, with \nbreezy winds across the Columbia Basin, Wenatchee Area, Waterville\nPlateau, West Plains, and Palouse. These winds also have the\npotential to pick up patchy blowing dust near recently plowed\nfields.\n\nWednesday through Monday: Models are in good agreement that a\ntransition from westerly flow to a strong upper ridge will occur\nlate in the week bringing about a warming trend. Yet there are a\ncouple of weak waves in the westerly flow that will track through\nWednesday and Thursday. The most likely scenario given the lack of\nlift is for passing mid and high clouds. Yet the NBM is carrying a\n20-30 percent chance of lingering light rain Wednesday morning in\nthe Central Panhandle Mountains, and then on Thursday a weak wave\nbringing a 20-30 percent chance of rain to the Cascades and a 5-10\npercent chance elsewhere. Once the ridge strengthens Friday into\nSaturday confidence increases of the region going into a prolonged\nwarm spell with dry conditions. The latest 6-10 and 8-14 day\noutlooks from the Climate Prediction Center also support this. JW",
